  "number": "6.11",
  "title": "Using the Quadratic Formula",
  "body": " Using the Quadratic Formula   Solving Equations Using the Quadratic Formula   Solving an Equation with Two Real Roots   Solve the equation using the Quadratic Formula.            The equation can be solved by substituting , , and into the Quadratic Formula .     Thus, the solutions are and .     Solving an Equation with One Real Root   Solve the equation using the Quadratic Formula.       Substitute , , and into .     Thus, the (repeated) solution is .     Solving an Equation with Two Complex Solutions   Solve the equation using the Quadratic Formula.       Substitute , , and into .     Thus, the solutions are and .      The Discriminant  The number and types of solutions for , , and can be summarized as follows.   The value of for is positive. This gave us two, real-valued solutions.    The value of for equals zero. This gave us a unique, real-valued solution.    The value of for is negative. This gave us a pair of complex numbers that are conjugates of one another.   The value of is called the discriminant since it discriminates between the different types of solutions a quadratic equation can have.   The Discriminant   We call the value the discriminant of the quadratic equation. We denote the discriminant by the letter , which gives us the equation .  In particular, the discriminant tells us the following information:    If , then the quadratic equation has two distinct, real-valued solutions.  If , then the quadratic equation has a unique, real-valued solution.  If , then the quadratic equation has a pair of complex-valued solutions that are complex conjugates of one another.       Use the Discriminant to Determine the Number and Types of Solutions, Version 1   Determine the number and type of solutions for the equation .    Two distinct, real-valued solutions.    In this case, we have , , and . We calculate the discriminant as follows.     Since , we have two distinct, real-valued solutions.     Use the Discriminant to Determine the Number and Types of Solutions, Version 2   Determine the number and type of solutions for the equation .    One unique, real-valued solution.    In this case, we have , , and . We calculate the discriminant as follows.     Since , we have a unique, real-valued solution.     Use the Discriminant to Determine the Number and Types of Solutions, Version 3   Determine the number and type of solutions for the equation .    Two complex-valued solutions that are complex conjugates of one another.    In this case, we have , , and . We calculate the discriminant as follows.     Since , the equation has two complex-valued solutions that are complex conjugates of one another.     "
